> Digital UNIX will allow me to use the same, or do I have to roll my own
> ?

I suggest that you use tpu-edt. It is well supported and does an
excellent job of emulating EDT keypad operations.
;; load tpu-edt emulator
(require 'tpu-edt)
(tpu-edt)
; tpu-set setup
(load "vt-control" t)
(setq require-final-newline t)
(define-key GOLD-SS3-map "P" 'universal-argument) ;Gold-PF1
(define-key SS3-map "w" 'tpu-paragraph)           ;KP7
(cond
 ('device_on_window-system-p
  (global-set-key [kp-7] 'tpu-paragraph)
  (define-key GOLD-map [kp-f1] 'universal-argument)))
(tpu-version)

The first time you run it, it will ask you to type the various keys
used so that it can correctly map them. This is saved and should not
require re-entry. It also includes several tpu capabilities like
rectangular cut and paste (tpu-toggle-rectangle) which are not
supported by EDT.

tpu-edt is a part of the standard Emacs and XEmacs distributions. (I
maintain the XEmacs version but Rod Riepel of Stanford wrote it and
does most of the work on maintaining it.)
